<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ignacy Łukasiewicz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1822">1822</a>–<a href="/years/1882">1882</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Jan Józef Ignacy Łukasiewicz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ignacy Łukasiewicz was a Polish pharmacist, engineer, businessman, inventor and philanthropist in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodomeria">Lodomeria</a>, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>. Born in Zaduszniki near Mielec, he was imprisoned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lwów</a> from 1846 to 1847 as a member of a party preparing an uprising, and graduated in pharmaceutics from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna">University of Vienna</a> in 1852. He discovered how to distill <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerosene">kerosene</a> from seep crude oil, invented the modern kerosene lamp in 1853, built the world's first modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oil-well">oil well</a> in 1854 and in 1856 opened the world's first industrial oil refinery at Ulaszowice, near Jasło. He died of pneumonia at Chorkówka in 1882.</p></div>
